1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,425
The Mystery of Antimatter.

2
00:00:02,375 --> 00:00:09,937
Every fundamental particle of matter has a corresponding antiparticle with identical mass but opposite charge and quantum numbers.

3
00:00:09,937 --> 00:00:24,987
The electron's antiparticle, the positron, carries a positive charge and was first predicted by Paul Dirac in 1928 through his relativistic wave equation; Carl Anderson confirmed its existence experimentally in cosmic rays in 1932.

4
00:00:24,987 --> 00:00:35,787
When a particle meets its antiparticle, they annihilate into pure energy — typically two or more gamma-ray photons — according to the mass-energy equivalence E = mc squared.

5
00:00:35,787 --> 00:00:43,775
The reverse process, pair production, converts high-energy photons into matter–antimatter pairs in the presence of a nucleus.

6
00:00:43,775 --> 00:00:49,425
Cosmological theory holds that the Big Bang produced equal quantities of matter and antimatter.

7
00:00:49,425 --> 00:01:02,975
Yet the observable universe consists almost entirely of matter, with antimatter observed only in minute quantities from cosmic rays, radioactive decay (such as potassium-40 producing positrons), and laboratory production.

8
00:01:02,975 --> 00:01:09,437
This asymmetry is known as baryogenesis and is one of the deepest open questions in fundamental physics.

9
00:01:09,437 --> 00:01:20,912
Experiments at CERN's Large Hadron Collider study charge-parity (CP) violation — slight differences in how matter and antimatter decay — which may account for the imbalance.

10
00:01:20,912 --> 00:01:31,150
The Standard Model's predicted CP violation is roughly ten orders of magnitude too small to explain the observed asymmetry, suggesting new physics beyond the Standard Model.

11
00:01:31,150 --> 00:01:46,487
Antimatter has practical applications: positron emission tomography (PET) scanners detect the 511 keV gamma rays from positron–electron annihilation to construct three-dimensional maps of metabolic activity, critical for oncology and neurology.

